.TH MC1-SLURM-PRESURVEY 1 "2026-10-06" "mu2edaq-batch-queues" "Mu2e DAQ Tools"
.SH NAME
mc1-slurm-presurvey \- read-only Slurm pre-install survey of the MC1 trigger worker nodes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mc1-slurm-presurvey
.RB [ \-j
.IR user@host ]
.RB [ \-u
.IR user ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR domain ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR sec ]
.RB [ \-\-gateway ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RI [ host ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mc1-slurm-presurvey
logs into each MC1 worker through the G2 gateway ProxyJump and runs
.IR scripts/lib/presurvey-remote.sh ,
which reports the facts a Slurm deployment depends on: OS and kernel,
installed Slurm/munge/MariaDB/PMIx packages, enabled dnf repositories and the
Slurm versions they offer, account resolution (local
.I /etc/passwd
versus SSSD) and the free system UID/GID range, configuration management
(Puppet/OpenVox) state, firewall state and rules, time synchronisation,
cgroup version and controllers, CPU/memory topology, filesystems and shared
mounts, forward resolution of all sixteen worker names, TCP reachability of the
peer workers, and running services.
.PP
The survey is read-only by intent. The one side effect is that
.B dnf list
refreshes repository metadata in the dnf cache of the login account.
.PP
One plain-text report per host is written to the output directory. Reports
contain enclave host detail and belong in the git-ignored
.I results/
tree; do not commit them.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-jump " " \fIuser@host\fR
ProxyJump hop into the enclave. Default
.BR mu2eshift@g2gateway01.fnal.gov .
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user " " \fIuser\fR
Login account on the target workers. Default
.BR root ,
which is needed to read firewall rules, sudoers and Puppet run state.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-domain " " \fIdomain\fR
Domain appended to host names without a dot. Default
.BR fnal.gov .
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-outdir " " \fIdir\fR
Report directory. Default
.IR results/presurvey-<UTC-stamp> .
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-timeout " " \fIsec\fR
ssh ConnectTimeout. Default 10.
.TP
.B \-\-gateway
Survey the jump host itself (as the jump account) instead of the workers.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print the ssh commands without connecting.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage.
.PP
Positional
.I host
arguments may be full names, short names, or bare numbers (\fB2\fR means
\fBmu2e-mc1-worker-02\fR). Default: workers 01, 02 and 03.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Each option has an environment override; the command line wins over the
environment, which wins over the built-in default.
.TP
.B MC1_JUMP
.TP
.B MC1_TARGET_USER
.TP
.B MC1_DOMAIN
.TP
.B MC1_OUTDIR
.TP
.B MC1_CONNECT_TIMEOUT
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the arguments parse; a failed host is reported as
.B FAILED
on stdout and its report holds the ssh error text.
.SH EXAMPLES
Survey workers 01\(en03 as root through the default jump:
.PP
.RS
.nf
klist
scripts/mc1-slurm-presurvey
.fi
.RE
.PP
Survey only worker-02 as mu2eshift, and the gateway:
.PP
.RS
.nf
scripts/mc1-slurm-presurvey -u mu2eshift 2
scripts/mc1-slurm-presurvey --gateway
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
A valid Kerberos ticket is required; authentication is GSSAPI through the
operator's ssh configuration. Claude Code's auto-mode classifier treats
logins to these production hosts as production reads; run the tool from
your own shell or with the
.B !
prefix inside a Claude Code session.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR dnf (8),
.BR slurm.conf (5)
